  "overview": {
    "text": "Mosaic is an art form made by arranging small pieces—often stone, ceramic, or glass—into patterns or images, usually set into mortar. The pieces are called tesserae; their colors, sizes, and placement let artists create geometric designs or picture-like scenes. Mosaics decorated floors and walls in many cultures. Roman examples, often found in homes and public buildings, could show stories, animals, daily activities, or abstract patterns, and their durability has preserved evidence of ancient tastes and spaces. In Byzantine art, glass mosaics became a striking feature of church interiors, where colored tesserae and gold backgrounds could transform ceilings and domes.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://www.metmuseum.org/de/perspectives/the-roman-mosaic-from-lod-israel",
      "https://www.getty.edu/publications/romanmosaics/introduction/",
      "https://www.metmuseum.org/pt/essays/glass-ornaments-in-late-antiquity-and-early-islam-ca-500-1000"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "Tesserae are the small pieces used to build a mosaic; Roman works could combine colored stone, terracotta, and glass.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.metmuseum.org/de/perspectives/the-roman-mosaic-from-lod-israel"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Roman mosaics decorated floors in private homes as well as public spaces, including baths and marketplaces.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.metmuseum.org/de/perspectives/the-roman-mosaic-from-lod-israel"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Some Roman mosaics used especially tiny tesserae to create detailed, painting-like pictures; these costly panels could be set within larger floor designs.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.metmuseum.org/de/perspectives/the-roman-mosaic-from-lod-israel",
        "https://www.getty.edu/publications/romanmosaics/introduction/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Mosaics could be designed for viewers walking over them, with images facing different directions or geometric patterns covering a floor.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.metmuseum.org/de/perspectives/the-roman-mosaic-from-lod-israel",
        "https://www.getty.edu/publications/romanmosaics/introduction/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Byzantine glass mosaics decorated church interiors; examples at Hagia Sophia include colored glass tesserae and gold backgrounds.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.metmuseum.org/pt/essays/glass-ornaments-in-late-antiquity-and-early-islam-ca-500-1000"
      ]
    }
  ],
